There's no existing project in the directory. Since you mentioned you have an existing project, here's the implementation you need — two pieces:

**1. Create `Middleware/ApiExceptionHandler.cs`:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourNamespace.Middleware;

internal sealed class ApiExceptionHandler(ILogger<ApiExceptionHandler> logger)
    : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (0, (string?)null)
        };

        if (statusCode == 0)
            return false; // Let the default handler produce a safe 500

        logger.LogWarning(exception, "Handled API exception: {Title}", title);

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = title,
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        }, cancellationToken);

        return true;
    }
}
```

**2. Register in `Program.cs`** (add before `var app = builder.Build()`):

```csharp
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ApiExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
```

Then after `builder.Build()`, before mapping endpoints:

```csharp
app.UseExceptionHandler();   // Catches exceptions → IExceptionHandler chain
app.UseStatusCodePages();    // Wraps bare status codes in Problem Details
```

**How it works:**

- `AddProblemDetails()` configures the default handler to return a generic 500 Problem Details response (no stack trace or exception message) for any exception your custom handler doesn't claim.
- Your `ApiExceptionHandler` handles the three known exception types by returning `true`.
- Returning `false` for unknown exceptions delegates to the default handler → safe 500.
- No try-catch needed in endpoints — just `throw new KeyNotFoundException()` etc.

**Example 500 response** (unhandled exception):

```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.6.1",
  "title": "An error occurred while processing your request.",
  "status": 500
}
```

No sensitive details are leaked because `Detail` is never set from `exception.Message` for unhandled cases.
